1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,777
Welcome to lectures three and four:

2
00:00:02,777 --> 00:00:04,839
data types and structures.

3
00:00:04,839 --> 00:00:18,960
We look at scales of measurement, discrete versus continuous data, structured and semi-structured data, the basics of text data, and the building blocks of time series behaviour.

4
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:28,852
Five building blocks for understanding how data is measured, organised, and behaves over time.

5
00:00:28,852 --> 00:00:29,239
One:

6
00:00:29,239 --> 00:00:33,494
nominal, ordinal, interval and ratio scales.

7
00:00:33,494 --> 00:00:33,881
Two:

8
00:00:33,881 --> 00:00:36,493
discrete versus continuous.

9
00:00:36,493 --> 00:00:37,073
Three:

10
00:00:37,073 --> 00:00:40,845
structured versus semi-structured data.

11
00:00:40,845 --> 00:00:41,329
Four:

12
00:00:41,329 --> 00:00:43,940
text data structure basics.

13
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:44,424
Five:

14
00:00:44,424 --> 00:00:48,776
time series behaviour, trend and seasonality.

15
00:00:49,576 --> 00:00:53,030
Levels of measurement start with categories.

16
00:00:53,030 --> 00:00:56,641
Nominal data has labels with no inherent rank:

17
00:00:56,641 --> 00:00:59,468
blood type, product colour, country.

18
00:00:59,468 --> 00:01:08,182
You can only say things are the same or different, so the allowed statistics are the mode and frequency counts.

19
00:01:08,182 --> 00:01:14,226
Ordinal data has a meaningful order but the gaps between ranks are not equal:

20
00:01:14,226 --> 00:01:19,643
satisfaction from low to high, education level, race finish position.

21
00:01:19,643 --> 00:01:23,176
Here you can also use the median and ranking.

22
00:01:23,976 --> 00:01:28,253
Both interval and ratio are numeric with equal gaps.

23
00:01:28,253 --> 00:01:31,707
The difference is whether zero means none.

24
00:01:31,707 --> 00:01:43,961
Interval data, like temperature in Celsius, calendar year, I Q score or pH, has an arbitrary zero, so ratio claims like twice as hot are not allowed.

25
00:01:43,961 --> 00:01:55,968
Ratio data, like height, weight, income, age and distance, has a true zero, so ratios and multiples are meaningful and all statistics are allowed.

26
00:01:56,768 --> 00:01:59,075
The four scales at a glance:

27
00:01:59,075 --> 00:02:04,102
nominal, ordinal, interval, ratio, with increasing precision.

28
00:02:04,102 --> 00:02:15,968
Nominal is named categories, ordinal is ordered categories, interval has equal gaps but no true zero, and ratio has equal gaps plus a true zero.

29
00:02:16,768 --> 00:02:20,477
Both discrete and continuous data are numeric.

30
00:02:20,477 --> 00:02:25,072
The difference is whether values are counted or measured.

31
00:02:25,072 --> 00:02:29,748
Discrete data are countable values, usually whole numbers:

32
00:02:29,748 --> 00:02:34,343
number of children, cars sold per day, number of defects.

33
00:02:34,343 --> 00:02:41,196
Continuous data can take any value in a range, limited only by measurement precision:

34
00:02:41,196 --> 00:02:44,824
height, temperature, time to complete a task.

35
00:02:45,624 --> 00:02:57,348
Structured data fits neatly into rows and columns with a fixed schema; every record has the same fields, like a spreadsheet or SQL table.

36
00:02:57,348 --> 00:03:06,675
Semi-structured data has tags or markers but no fixed table schema, so fields can vary from record to record.

37
00:03:06,675 --> 00:03:14,976
Examples are J S O N and X M L files, emails with headers plus free text, and no S Q L documents.

38
00:03:15,776 --> 00:03:21,684
Raw text has no rows or columns of its own, so it is broken into layers.

39
00:03:21,684 --> 00:03:25,541
The corpus is the full collection of documents.

40
00:03:25,541 --> 00:03:29,234
A document is one unit, like a single review.

41
00:03:29,234 --> 00:03:35,142
A token is a word, number or punctuation mark, produced by tokenisation.

42
00:03:35,142 --> 00:03:39,902
The vocabulary is the set of unique tokens after cleaning.

43
00:03:39,902 --> 00:03:53,360
Typical prep steps are lowercasing, removing stopwords, and stemming or lemmatisation, which turn free text into structured features such as a term frequency table.

44
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:58,173
A time series is data recorded at regular intervals.

45
00:03:58,173 --> 00:04:02,572
Two patterns to look for first are trend and seasonality.

46
00:04:02,572 --> 00:04:13,609
Trend is the long-term direction, upward, downward or flat, once short-term noise is smoothed out; look for a rising or falling moving average.

47
00:04:13,609 --> 00:04:24,568
Seasonality is a pattern that repeats at fixed intervals, such as every twelve months, driven by predictable factors like weather or holidays.

48
00:04:25,368 --> 00:04:27,056
Bringing it together.

49
00:04:27,056 --> 00:04:33,083
The measurement scale determines which statistics and operations are valid.

50
00:04:33,083 --> 00:04:37,423
Discrete data is counted; continuous data is measured.

51
00:04:37,423 --> 00:04:44,093
Structured data fits a fixed schema, while semi-structured data uses flexible tags.

52
00:04:44,093 --> 00:04:50,040
Text must be broken into corpus, document, token and term before analysis.

53
00:04:50,040 --> 00:04:58,800
And in time series, separate the steady trend from the repeating seasonal pattern before drawing conclusions.

